{"severity":"warning","file":"errors/copy_shared_1.march","start_line":9,"start_col":22,"end_line":9,"end_col":24,"message":"`List.map` builds a new copy instead of reusing `xs` in place, because `xs` is used again afterwards.\nA function can update a value in place only when it holds the only reference to it; make this call the last use of `xs` and `List.map` reuses its memory instead of allocating.\nhelp: move `let n = List.length(xs)` above the call.","code":"copy_shared","fix":{"kind":"multi","edits":[{"kind":"delete","start_line":10,"end_line":10},{"kind":"insert","after_line":8,"text":"    let n = List.length(xs)"}],"applicability":"machine-applicable"},"labels":[{"file":"errors/copy_shared_1.march","start_line":10,"start_col":24,"end_line":10,"end_col":26,"message":"`xs` is used again here, so it is still shared when `List.map` runs"}],"notes":[],"category":"COPY"}
